WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.450
When we talked about JCL,

2
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.290
we mentioned that it can be used to call a program.

3
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:09.930
That's something you're probably pretty familiar

4
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.490
with running a program.

5
00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.130
JCL can also call a procedure.

6
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.330
Now, a procedure is a set of frequently used JCL statements

7
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.610
that have been grouped together and named

8
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.490
so they can be invoked using a single name.

9
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.590
Most JCL procedures perform a general function,

10
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.950
which allows them to be called from a variety of jobs.

11
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.610
And that just makes a whole lot of sense

12
00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.330
from an efficiency standpoint.

13
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.290
It's easier and cleaner to reference a procedure

14
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.970
than a series of statements,

15
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.910
especially if they're gonna be used the same way each time.

16
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.370
Procedures are usually stored in system data sets.

17
00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.290
That makes them the procedure libraries,

18
00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:53.360
or proc lib for short.

19
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.160
You can think of a procedure like a play

20
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.200
that a football team might have.

21
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.640
All of the movements and the details are all set

22
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.160
and rehearsed ahead of time,

23
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.080
and you just have to call it by name.

24
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.280
That makes the proc lib the playbook,

25
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.240
a collection of commonly used steps,

26
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.880
each with its own name.

27
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.040
When a procedure is placed into proc lib,

28
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.780
it can be made available to a wider audience.

29
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.620
And it can also be referenced simply by name

30
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.960
instead of specifying the full path each time.

31
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.960
However, procedures can also be stored

32
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.420
in private proc libs or even within a job.

33
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.440
This restricts who can call them,

34
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.780
and sometimes that's by design.

35
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.600
There are two types of procedures,

36
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.000
catalog procedures and in-stream procedures.

37
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.640
Both types are invoked by using

38
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.960
one of the following JCL statements.

39
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.680
You can say step name exec proc equals procedure name,

40
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.000
or step name exec procedure name.

41
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.440
You can see how this works here,

42
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.220
and it spells out some of the benefits of using a procedure.

43
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.320
All these steps and everything that connects it all together,

44
00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.640
it's now all within this single procedure,

45
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.920
and a programmer can very easily reference

46
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.880
that one procedure.

47
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.580
Here's an example of an in-stream procedure.

48
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.420
So we've got this line up here saying here's our procedure,

49
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.180
and below it here's step one,

50
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.580
which is going to execute a program called APGM.

51
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.740
And the whole thing goes between the proc and P-E-N-D,

52
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.720
procedure end statement.

53
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.040
This is our in-stream procedure.

54
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.260
If you're using a catalog procedure,

55
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.260
you don't have to code the proc and P-N statements.

56
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.220
However, if an in-stream proc statement

57
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.780
is coded into a catalog procedure,

58
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.060
then it has to be the procedure's first statement.

59
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:36.900
You can see here,

60
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.260
once we've defined that in-stream procedure by name,

61
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.500
we can reference it in later steps.

62
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.180
And as you can also see, like we've mentioned before,

63
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.620
there are two ways of calling it.

64
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.460
You can also see that when you reference a procedure,

65
00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.420
you could be referencing an in-stream procedure,

66
00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.460
one in a system proc lib, or one in a private proc lib.

67
00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.900
Now, if the procedure is in a private procedure library,

68
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.940
that library needs to be specified

69
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.940
on the job's JCL lib statement,

70
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.220
which needs to appear before the exec statement.

71
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.060
And if the called procedure is cataloged in a system library,

72
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.300
it will be retrieved from the sys1.proc lib,

73
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.800
or another defined procedure library.

74
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.380
If it's not in any of those,

75
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.100
then it won't be able to run your specified procedure.

76
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.640
It can't find it.

77
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.720
About that JCL lib statement,

78
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.320
we mentioned that it can be used to define private proc libs.

79
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.340
It can also be used to determine the order

80
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.760
in which the proc libs should be searched.

81
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.640
So if you specify proc libs in your JCL lib statement,

82
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.120
it will look for those before it looks

83
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.220
in the default system procedure library.

84
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.060
You may only need to specify one proc lib here,

85
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.460
or if you need to specify two or more,

86
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.040
just separate them by commas

87
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.320
and put them inside parentheses.

88
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.040
You only get one JCL lib statement per job,

89
00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.640
and it must appear after the job statement

90
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.460
and before the first exec statement.

91
00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.660
We have another useful statement called include.

92
00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.100
If you have a series of JCL statements coded

93
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.860
within a member of a PDS,

94
00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.760
you can insert them into JCL using an include statement.

95
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.000
So here's the include statement.

96
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.920
It's got the name of the include, the statement include,

97
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.080
and then the dataset name.

98
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.560
When the JCL is being read, it gets there

99
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.820
and it just replaces it

100
00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.480
with all the contents of that dataset.

101
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.840
Where this comes in handy is when you need to define

102
00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.760
a whole bunch of DD statements for a series of JCLs.

103
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.720
Now, you could just copy and paste them,

104
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.520
but then you would also have to go around fixing all of them

105
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.360
every single time you wanted to make a change

106
00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.160
to those DD statements.

107
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.880
It's a whole lot easier to write them once,

108
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.520
so you can reference them with an include statement.

109
00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.360
And if there's updates to be made,

110
00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.940
you only have to make those updates in one place.

111
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.240
A couple of limitations on procedures and includes.

112
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.260
Procedures can be nested up to a maximum of 15 levels.

113
00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.860
So that's a procedure, calling a procedure,

114
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.080
calling a procedure, calling a procedure,

115
00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.960
and you get the idea.

116
00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.280
So you get some flexibility,

117
00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.200
but you can't just go nuts with procedures

118
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.400
referencing other procedures.

119
00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.920
Same thing with includes.

120
00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.520
You get up to a maximum of 15 levels there as well.

121
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.160
A procedure can reference an include group,

122
00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.060
and an include group can access a procedure.

123
00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.520
So what you're seeing here is an example

124
00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.140
of a procedure called eval,

125
00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.240
which has a procedure named EPRT nested within it.

126
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.080
In addition, the procedure EPRT has an include group

127
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.920
called genRDD nested within it.

128
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.880
In JCL, we can set what's called symbolic parameters.

129
00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.060
Symbolic parameters, usually just called symbolics,

130
00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.220
can be assigned through three statements,

131
00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.260
proc statements, exec statements, and set statements.

132
00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.860
This means you can define a symbolic parameter,

133
00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.280
assign it a value,

134
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.180
and then you can just reference that symbolic later on.

135
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.920
So the next time you run the JCL,

136
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.220
if you want to run it with a different value in there,

137
00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.640
just change the symbolic definition.

138
00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.180
You get to define the symbolic names and values.

139
00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.720
In addition to the symbolic parameters that you define,

140
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.060
there are a number of system symbols,

141
00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.700
and these come in two flavors.

142
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.740
There's dynamic system symbols,

143
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.620
whose values can change at any point after an IPL,

144
00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.740
like current, date, and time.

145
00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.660
The full set of dynamic system symbols is fixed,

146
00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.940
and you cannot define additional ones.

147
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.140
What you have is what you get.

148
00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.300
There's also static system symbols.

149
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.620
These are set at IPL time,

150
00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:43.060
and won't change until the next time the system IPLs.

151
00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.380
So for example, the system name and systplex name.

152
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.980
Static system symbols have two types.

153
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.380
There's syst and define static symbols,

154
00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.700
and these include ampersand sysclone, systname, systplex,

155
00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.860
sys-r1, and syst-a-l-v-l.

156
00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.620
That's used for the system architecture level.

157
00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.200
Then there's installation defined static symbols,

158
00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.260
which your system programmer, maybe that's you,

159
00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.380
gets to set.

160
00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.900
These are defined in the syst1.parmlib dataset,

161
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.300
IEA sim member.

162
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.420
In the example here,

163
00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.180
you'll see a mix of JCL symbols in red,

164
00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.660
and system symbols in blue.

165
00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.980
Note that you can use static system symbols,

166
00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:27.660
such as systname in started task JCL procedures,

167
00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.940
but not in regular batch JCL.

168
00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:34.220
Overriding a parameter modifies only the one parameter.

169
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.280
All the other parameters stay unaffected,

170
00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.360
unless you modify them as well.

171
00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.900
So if you override the dataset name on a DD statement,

172
00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.320
which includes the unit and vol equals seer parameters,

173
00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.400
it'll change the dataset name,

174
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.760
but the unit and vol equals seer parameters

175
00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.040
will remain unchanged.

176
00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.040
But you can't correct invalid parameters

177
00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.920
in a procedure through overrides,

178
00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.840
because before doing any substitutions,

179
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.880
the system scans the original procedure statements

180
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.780
and issues error messages based on those.

181
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:09.320
So it's got to be valid before you issue overrides.

182
00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.740
If you made a mistake while you were creating a procedure

183
00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:15.280
and you typed AL instead of all, we've all been there,

184
00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.040
you can't substitute your way out of that situation.

185
00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.960
Lastly, you can override the output JCL and DD statements

186
00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.080
by specifying the step for which they apply.

187
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.400
If no step has been named previously,

188
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.360
the changes apply to the very first step.

189
00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:32.900
If you have specified a step

190
00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.320
and you want to change a couple more output JCL

191
00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.700
and DD statements,

192
00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:39.700
you only have to specify it the first time.

193
00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:40.960
The ones that follow it,

194
00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.200
it just assumes it's in the same one as above.

195
00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.520
And there's your introduction to JCL procedures.

196
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.160
We got into some fairly tricky concepts there.

197
00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.480
So don't worry if you feel a little lost,

198
00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:54.080
overwhelmed or intimidated.

199
00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.560
I mean, I'm looking at my camera operator right now

200
00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.120
and he looks like he's about to pass out.

201
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.080
So I'm gonna go check on him

202
00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.520
and I'll see you in the next lesson, all right?

203
00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:03.800
Hey man, you all right?

204
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:04.640
You doing all right?
